# Jittered and dodged positions: position_jitterdodge

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`position_jitterdodge()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/position_jitterdodge.html), in
the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An
example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the
page is also a list of what remains.

`position_jitterdodge()` dodges and jitters at once. It is mostly for lining points up with
boxplots dodged side by side, as they are when a boxplot maps `fill`: each point moves to its
group's place in the dodge, then by a little random noise, sideways only unless `jitter.height`
says otherwise. Giggly dodges bars, with `position: :dodge` on `Giggly.Plot.geom_bar/3`, but has
none of this yet: no jitter, no boxplots, and no dodging of points.

## Points over dodged boxplots

```r
set.seed(596)
dsub <- diamonds[sample(nrow(diamonds), 1000), ]
ggplot(dsub, aes(x = cut, y = carat, fill = clarity)) +
  geom_boxplot(outlier.size = 0) +
  geom_point(pch = 21, position = position_jitterdodge())
```

R's `sample()` picks diamonds at random, the same ones every time after `set.seed(596)`;
`pch = 21` is the point shape with both a border and a fill.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_boxplot()` and its `outlier.size` option, and `position_jitterdodge()`.